<Base title="Biology — Zuhaib Ul Zamann" description="Procedural 3D studies of living mechanisms. Each one is driven by a single degree of freedom: watch the heart's valves obey pressure, the eye refocus, and a cell export a protein.">
  <nav class="crumbs"><a href="/visuals">Visual laboratory</a><span>/</span><span>Biology</span></nav>
  <MechanismStudy />
  <section class="study-notes">
    <h2>How these are built</h2>
    <p>No meshes are downloaded. Every surface is generated in code when the page loads. The heart's chambers are lofted from cross-sections, its valve leaflets are parametric sheets hinged on their rings, and its vessels are swept tubes. The eye's cornea, lens, iris and ciliary body are surfaces of revolution about the optical axis. The cell's ER and Golgi are membrane sacs laid over curved sheets. Noise fields roughen and colour the tissue throughout.</p>
    <p>Each model is then driven by one number. In the heart it is position in the cardiac cycle: ventricular volume warps the myocardium, and each valve opens only when the pressure behind it exceeds the pressure ahead. In the eye it is the distance of the target. The ciliary muscle contracts, the zonular fibres slacken and the lens rounds up, or in the presbyopic eye mostly fails to. In the cell it is time since a cohort of protein was made. Seventy-two molecules are followed from the rough ER through the Golgi to the membrane, either fusing as they arrive or waiting in granules for a calcium signal.</p>
    <p>The traces follow standard textbook physiology, with timing and scale chosen for teaching rather than measured from one person. Geometry is a stylised reconstruction: proportions and relationships are anatomical, but no single specimen was traced. The eye's focusing error is drawn six times larger than life so the blur can be seen, and its lens powers are approximate textbook values. The cell's organelles and vesicles are not to scale, an hour of transport is compressed into one cycle, and the lumen pH values are typical figures for each compartment. Sources: <a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-3-cardiac-cycle">the cardiac cycle</a> and <a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/14-1-sensory-perception">sensory perception</a> in OpenStax Anatomy &amp; Physiology, and <a href="https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins">the endomembrane system</a> in OpenStax Biology.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

